Below is a clean, production-ready way to build a separate TypeScript module that exports React components, compatible with React 19.2.3 and modern bundlers.

I’ll assume:
	•	You want a reusable package (component library style)
	•	Written in TypeScript
	•	Consumed by another React app
	•	No framework lock-in (works with Vite, Next, CRA, etc.)

⸻

1. Project Structure (Recommended)

my-react-module/
├─ src/
│  ├─ components/
│  │  └─ Button.tsx
│  ├─ index.ts
├─ package.json
├─ tsconfig.json
├─ tsconfig.build.json
└─ README.md


⸻

2. Install Dependencies

Runtime deps (⚠️ React should be peer dependency)

npm install react@19.2.3 react-dom@19.2.3

Dev deps

npm install -D typescript @types/react @types/react-dom


⸻

3. package.json (Very Important)

{
  "name": "@your-scope/my-react-module",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/index.js",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs",
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
    }
  },
  "files": ["dist"],
  "peerDependencies": {
    "react": "^19.2.3",
    "react-dom": "^19.2.3"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc -p tsconfig.build.json"
  }
}

Why peerDependencies?

So the consuming app controls the React version → avoids multiple React copies.

⸻

4. TypeScript Config

tsconfig.json (Base)

{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}

tsconfig.build.json (Library Output)

{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "declaration": true,
    "emitDeclarationOnly": false,
    "outDir": "dist",
    "rootDir": "src",
    "module": "ESNext"
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}


⸻

5. Example React Component

src/components/Button.tsx

import { type ReactNode } from "react";

export interface ButtonProps {
  children: ReactNode;
  onClick?: () => void;
}

export function Button({ children, onClick }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onClick} style={{ padding: "8px 12px" }}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}


⸻

6. Public Entry Point

src/index.ts

export * from "./components/Button";

This is critical — never export deep internal paths.

⸻

7. Build

npm run build

You’ll get:

dist/
├─ index.js
├─ index.d.ts


⸻

8. Usage in Another React App

npm install @your-scope/my-react-module

import { Button } from "@your-scope/my-react-module";

export function App() {
  return <Button onClick={() => alert("Hello")}>Click me</Button>;
}


⸻

9. React 19 Notes (Important)

React 19:
	•	Uses automatic JSX runtime
	•	No need to import React in every file
	•	Types are bundled properly with @types/react@19

Make sure consumer app also uses:

"jsx": "react-jsx"


⸻

