Great — for Next.js (Pages Router) there are a few non-obvious constraints you should follow to make your separated React 19 module work perfectly.

Below is a battle-tested setup specifically for Next.js Pages Router consuming a TypeScript React component library.

⸻

Key Next.js Pages Router Constraints
	1.	Single React instance (peerDependencies is mandatory)
	2.	ESM output must be transpiled by Next
	3.	No window / browser APIs at module top-level
	4.	CSS must be imported explicitly
	5.	Server/Client boundary awareness

⸻

1. Library Setup (Adjusted for Next.js)

package.json

{
  "name": "@your-scope/ui",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "./dist/index.js",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
      "import": "./dist/index.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    }
  },
  "peerDependencies": {
    "react": "^19.2.3",
    "react-dom": "^19.2.3"
  }
}

✔ Do NOT bundle React
✔ Do NOT mark React as dependency

⸻

2. Build Tool (Recommended: tsup)

Next.js Pages Router does not transpile node_modules by default unless configured.

Using tsup gives:
	•	ESM + CJS
	•	.d.ts
	•	Next-friendly output

Install

npm install -D tsup

tsup.config.ts

import { defineConfig } from "tsup";

export default defineConfig({
  entry: ["src/index.ts"],
  format: ["esm", "cjs"],
  dts: true,
  sourcemap: true,
  clean: true,
  external: ["react", "react-dom"],
});

Build

npx tsup


⸻

3. React Component (Next-Safe)

Button.tsx

import { type ReactNode } from "react";

export interface ButtonProps {
  children: ReactNode;
  onClick?: () => void;
}

export function Button({ children, onClick }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button
      onClick={onClick}
      style={{ padding: 8 }}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}

🚫 NO useEffect that accesses window without guard
🚫 NO top-level browser API calls

⸻

4. Entry Point

src/index.ts

export * from "./components/Button";


⸻

5. Consume in Next.js Pages Router

Install

npm install @your-scope/ui


⸻

6. Configure Next.js to Transpile the Package

Required for ESM libraries

next.config.js

/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  transpilePackages: ["@your-scope/ui"],
};

module.exports = nextConfig;

⚠️ Without this, you’ll see:
	•	Unexpected token 'export'
	•	Module parse failed
	•	Cannot use import statement outside a module

⸻

7. Use in a Page

pages/index.tsx

import { Button } from "@your-scope/ui";

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <Button onClick={() => alert("Hello from library")}>
        Click me
      </Button>
    </main>
  );
}


⸻

8. CSS Support (Important)

Library

// src/components/Button.css
.button {
  padding: 8px;
}

import "./Button.css";

export function Button() {
  return <button className="button">Click</button>;
}

Next.js

CSS must be imported from _app.tsx:

// pages/_app.tsx
import "@your-scope/ui/dist/style.css";

✔ Required by Pages Router rules

⸻

9. SSR Safety Checklist

Your library MUST NOT:

❌ Access window, document, localStorage at module scope
❌ Assume browser-only execution
❌ Use useLayoutEffect without fallback

Safe Pattern

const isBrowser = typeof window !== "undefined";


⸻

10. React 19 Compatibility Notes
	•	jsx: react-jsx required
	•	No legacy JSX transform
	•	No React.FC required
	•	Types from @types/react@19 only

⸻

11. Common Next.js Errors & Fixes

Error	Fix
Invalid hook call	React in peerDependencies
export not found	Rebuild library
Unexpected token export	transpilePackages
Hydration mismatch	SSR-unsafe logic


